# BugBarn Integration: golden-app

You are an AI assistant. This page contains everything needed to integrate BugBarn
error tracking into the "golden-app" project. Read it carefully and implement accordingly.

## Project configuration

| Field     | Value |
|-----------|-------|
| Endpoint  | https://bb.customer.nl |
| Project   | golden-app |
| API Key   | 9f6423dde78dfde91f25a2c5fe171754b8b0926e |
| Status    | new |

## Pending admin approval

New projects are created pending approval. Send events now — they are accepted
(HTTP 202) and queued. They are ingested into the dashboard once an admin
approves the project.

## What BugBarn captures

- **Exceptions** — unhandled errors with stack traces, grouped by fingerprint
- **Structured logs** — INFO/WARN/ERROR log lines with arbitrary key-value data
- **Release markers** — deploy events that correlate releases with regressions

## HTTP API — works in any language

Send errors via POST. Returns 202 immediately (async processing).

~~~bash
curl -X POST https://bb.customer.nl/api/v1/events \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -H "X-BugBarn-Api-Key: 9f6423dde78dfde91f25a2c5fe171754b8b0926e" \
  -H "X-BugBarn-Project: golden-app" \
  -d '{
    "severityText": "error",
    "body": "something went wrong",
    "exception": {
      "type": "RuntimeError",
      "message": "something went wrong",
      "stacktrace": [
        {"function": "main.run", "filename": "main.go", "lineno": 42}
      ]
    },
    "attributes": { "environment": "production" }
  }'
~~~

## TypeScript / Node.js

Install (the SDK is self-hosted — fetch the tarball URL from the package registry first):
~~~bash
# Step 1: get the tarball URL
curl -s https://bb.customer.nl/packages/typescript/latest.json
# → {"version":"...","url":"/packages/typescript/bugbarn-typescript-<hash>.tgz"}

# Step 2: install using the full URL from the "url" field above
npm install https://bb.customer.nl/packages/typescript/<tarball-filename>.tgz
# or: pnpm add https://bb.customer.nl/packages/typescript/<tarball-filename>.tgz
~~~

Usage:
~~~typescript
import { init, captureException } from "@bugbarn/typescript";

init({
  apiKey: "9f6423dde78dfde91f25a2c5fe171754b8b0926e",
  endpoint: "https://bb.customer.nl",
  project: "golden-app",
});

// Capture an error
try {
  riskyOperation();
} catch (err) {
  captureException(err);
}
~~~

## Go

~~~bash
go get github.com/wiebe-xyz/bugbarn-go
~~~

~~~go
import bb "github.com/wiebe-xyz/bugbarn-go"

func main() {
    bb.Init(bb.Options{
        APIKey:      "9f6423dde78dfde91f25a2c5fe171754b8b0926e",
        Endpoint:    "https://bb.customer.nl",
        ProjectSlug: "golden-app",
        Environment: "production",
    })
    defer bb.Shutdown(5 * time.Second)

    if err := doSomething(); err != nil {
        bb.CaptureError(err)
    }
}

// For HTTP servers — catches panics and reports them:
http.ListenAndServe(":8080", bb.RecoverMiddleware(yourHandler))
~~~

## Python

The Python SDK is not yet published to PyPI. Install from source:
~~~bash
pip install "git+https://github.com/webwiebe/bugbarn.git#subdirectory=sdks/python"
~~~

~~~python
from bugbarn import init, capture_exception

init(
    api_key="9f6423dde78dfde91f25a2c5fe171754b8b0926e",
    endpoint="https://bb.customer.nl",
    install_excepthook=True,  # auto-capture unhandled exceptions
)

# Note: project routing is by API key, not an SDK option.
# Use X-BugBarn-Project header directly if you need multi-project routing.

try:
    risky_operation()
except Exception as e:
    capture_exception(e)
~~~

## Release marker — send on every deploy

~~~bash
curl -X POST https://bb.customer.nl/api/v1/releases \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -H "X-BugBarn-Api-Key: 9f6423dde78dfde91f25a2c5fe171754b8b0926e" \
  -H "X-BugBarn-Project: golden-app" \
  -d '{
    "name": "v1.2.3",
    "environment": "production",
    "version": "v1.2.3",
    "commitSha": "'$(git rev-parse HEAD)'",
    "url": "https://github.com/your-org/your-repo/releases/tag/v1.2.3",
    "notes": "Deployed via CI"
  }'
~~~

## Send structured logs

~~~bash
curl -X POST https://bb.customer.nl/api/v1/logs \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -H "X-BugBarn-Api-Key: 9f6423dde78dfde91f25a2c5fe171754b8b0926e" \
  -H "X-BugBarn-Project: golden-app" \
  -d '{"level": "warn", "message": "cache miss", "key": "user:42", "ttl": 300}'
~~~

## Need a new key for an existing project?

An agent can ask for one; an admin approves it in the dashboard. The request
holds no key. The key is minted on the one poll that follows the approval.

~~~bash
# 1. ask (label = repo and environment, shown to the admin)
curl -s -X POST https://bb.customer.nl/api/v1/setup/golden-app/key-requests \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"label": "my-org/my-repo production"}'
# -> {"id":"...","pollSecret":"...","status":"pending","expiresAt":"..."}

# 2. poll until an admin approves (requests expire after 24 hours)
curl -s https://bb.customer.nl/api/v1/setup/golden-app/key-requests/<id> \
  -H "X-BugBarn-Poll-Secret: <pollSecret>"
# -> {"status":"pending"} | {"status":"denied"} | HTTP 410 {"status":"expired"}
# -> {"status":"approved","apiKey":"..."} exactly once, then {"status":"claimed"}
~~~

Or with the CLI, which waits for the approval and writes the key to stdout only
when asked: `bb key-request golden-app --label my-org/my-repo --output-secret | gh secret set BUGBARN_API_KEY`
The key is project-scoped and can post events and release markers.

